The image's FreeRTOS port is the SDK's nRF52 CMSIS port with the kernel's own
Cortex-M4F primitives put back in place of the CMSIS-derived ones the SDK
substituted. Each hunk is one of those, and each is decided by a body that stops
differing once it is there.

portmacro_cmsis.h
  Interrupt masking goes through CMSIS's __set_BASEPRI, a plain `msr basepri`
  off a C constant. Every body in the image that enters a critical section shows
  the kernel's asm form instead: the mask materialised with `mov` (not `movs`)
  and `isb; dsb` after the write. vPortEnterCritical (0x73e3c) is the whole of it
  in five instructions and xQueueGiveFromISR (0x9e828) shows the read-modify-
  write variant with `mrs` first. This is inlined everywhere, so it is most of
  the difference: it settles nine queue and task bodies at once.

  portYIELD sends an event as well as pending PendSV. No caller in the image
  does: xTaskResumeAll (0x73276) pends, fences and nothing else. The tick ISR
  does send one, but out of its own source, not out of this macro.

port_cmsis.c
  PendSV's priority is set with CMSIS's NVIC_SetPriority, which stores one byte.
  The image (0x74080) reads, ors and writes the whole system-handler priority
  word, which is what the kernel's own port does.

  vPortEnableVFP is C, so GCC inlines it into xPortStartScheduler. The image
  calls it (0x74078 -> 0x73dfc) and the body at 0x73dfc is the kernel's naked
  asm version verbatim, 0xE000ED88 in its own literal pool.

  prvTaskExitError ends in for(;;). The image (inlined at 0x740b6) spins on a
  volatile zero, which is what the kernel's own version does so a debugger can
  write the variable and step out.

port.c
  vPortStartFirstTask sets MSP and enables interrupts; the image (0x73dd0)
  clears CONTROL between the two, so the first task starts privileged on the
  main stack with no lazy FP context inherited from whatever ran before.

  xPortPendSVHandler saves r3 alone across vTaskSwitchContext; the image
  (0x27e6c) saves r0 and r3. Both the mnemonic and the register list matter:
  `stmdb sp!` assembles wide and `push` narrow, and the image has the wide one
  going in and the narrow `pop` coming out, which is exactly what the kernel's
  source text produces.

  Both handlers end their asm with `.align 2`. The image's literal pools sit
  four bytes further on, on 16-byte boundaries, which is `.align 4`.

